on this morning's dish Final Four  edition from the host city of  Minneapolis thousands of basketball fans  have descended on the Twin Cities for  this weekend's games and some may be  looking for a good place to eat I'm sure  they are well Dana visited with one of  the hottest chefs in town Dana tell us  about her yeah the lucky fans around  here are visiting restaurants run by  Chef and Kim she's made a name for  herself by taking the distinctive  flavors of her Korean heritage and  applying them in some unexpected ways  tell me about where we're sitting yeah  so this is young Joanie  if you come to eat here it's a  culmination of my palate what I love to  eat I think you can really feel what my  heart and soul is it doesn't take long  to cook I know I bet chef Anne Kim's  heart and soul at young Joanie is part  DNA part ingenuity this is a sampling of  what we offer here at young Joanie and  the things that I chose is really a  reflection of my upbringing the dishes  include pork belly song the Korean  weren't for rash sometimes it gets a  little mess I would say I've completely  overstuffed Korean barbecue short ribs  we grill it on our woodfired grill add  chimichurri and on the base of it is a  charred scallion creme fraiche and pizza  with beef topped with go-to Jeong  vinaigrette salad help yourself  through her food and Kim tells personal  stories this is a childhood favorite of  mine my mother used to stick Japanese  sweet potatoes in our fireplace when we  were little and we take him out of the  coals unwrap them and eat them like  candy and I loved that treat Kim  immigrated to the United States in the  late 1970s when she was just four years  old it's really my maternal grandmother  who raised us because both my parents  worked sort of graveyard shifts I was  always by her side when she was cooking  I always wanted to help I always wanted  to stir the pot when something was  boiling and it was just a part of my my  upbringing growing up she says she was a  class clown her parents worked multiple  jobs and wanted a better life for her as  a doctor or lawyer for college  Kim headed to New York City  when I got into Columbia I thought okay  the parents are good with that but my  ulterior motive was I was gonna start  acting and someday my name would be up  in lights and I'd be on Broadway and so  ironically I got my headshots done in  New York and I moved back to Minneapolis  and I started an eight-year acting  career back home Kim appeared in  productions ranging from Shakespeare's  Othello to promotional videos hello  everyone and welcome to dream date but  she got tired of the uncertainty of  acting when in 2007 her boyfriend now  husband Conrad laugher lost his job in  finance I was looking to make a change  he suggested Kim open a restaurant  I'm the risk-taker and my job was  basically to push an over the over the  cliff I tend to like jump in and then  freak out after I mean that's what he  did for the majority of his life is he  kind of made that Sun yeah on the market  and things and me I played it safe Kim's  first idea was to open a franchise of  the sandwich chain Jimmy John's and  that's what I thought I was going to do  until a lawyer that we hired said during  this whole meeting you never once talked  about your excitement for this Jimmy  John's but how some day you hope that to  open up this intimate family like  restaurant and in good faith I can't I  can't do this for you so he said you  should reconsider doing your own thing  Kim missed New York Pizza and wanted to  bring it to Minneapolis she began  reading books on baking before studying  in San Francisco under Tony Gemignani  13-time world Pizza champion you hear  that crunch after Kim and laugher walked  their dog Lola by an empty convenience  store in 2009 they knew where they would  open their first restaurant they emptied  their savings and maxed out their credit  cards to open pizzeria Lola this is the  only restaurant we actually worked in  yeah scheduled on the line Conrad was a  hoax yeah I promoted myself out of that  job when we opened it was pretty much  all hands on deck and and made pretty  much every pizza yeah it was a lot of  work there's a lot of hours there was a  lot of fun a little more than two years  later they opened hello Pizza a New York  style slice shop like Kim remembered  from college but their most ambitious  project is young Joanie named after each  of their mothers the roughly 7,000  square foot restaurant includes a back  bar speakeasy modeled after lay furs  family cabin in North Dakota creativity  has always driven me to me I just left  one stage the theater stage for another  stage Kim the aspiring actress turned  professional chef was recently named a  semifinalist for the James Beard Awards  after being a finalist one year ago they  call it the Oscars of food so if I  wasn't gonna get that Oscar on the stage  or behind the screen I was gonna get it  you know hopefully get it somehow all of  those things are really really nice and  it's an honor but it's really about the  people that fill the seats and love what  we do all right now I'm craving in Kim's  pizza
 

